# Shell scripts and git hooks must stay LF — a CRLF (\r) breaks them under /bin/sh
# on macOS/Linux (and Git for Windows runs hooks through sh).
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
# The install line's template is `include_str!`'d and substituted into the
# script the feed serves, so a CRLF checkout here ships a `#!/bin/sh\r` that
# every Linux box refuses. `*.sh` does not match a `.sh.in`.
packaging/**/*.in text eol=lf

# The web client's build output, committed on purpose: `include_dir!` embeds it at
# compile time so `cargo install lait` needs no npm (docs/SERVE.md). Mark it
# generated so it collapses in diffs and stays out of language stats — review the
# source in `viewer/`, never this. LF-pinned so the bundle is byte-identical
# across platforms and CI's rebuild-and-diff check doesn't fire on line endings.
src/serve/assets/** linguist-generated=true
src/serve/assets/** text eol=lf
# ...except the fonts. The rule above exists for the JS/CSS bundle, and applied
# to a woff2 it strips every 0x0D byte that happens to fall inside the compressed
# stream — `inter-latin-ext-wght-normal.woff2` lost 3 bytes that way and would
# have been checked out corrupt on every other machine. `binary` is -text -diff.
src/serve/assets/*.woff2 binary
# Vite copies this file through into the bundle, so its line endings end up in
# `src/serve/assets/index.html`. Checked out as CRLF on Windows, the HTML
# transform re-joins a line that already ends in a carriage return and emits a
# doubled one; the `eol=lf` rule above then collapses only the pair, leaving
# exactly one CRLF in the committed blob. A Linux build emits a bare newline
# there, so CI's rebuild-and-diff fired on every commit made from Windows — the
# same one line, forever. Pinning the *source* to LF is the fix: with no
# carriage return to double, both platforms build the same bytes.
viewer/index.html text eol=lf
# `npm ci` needs the lockfile byte-stable across platforms.
viewer/package-lock.json text eol=lf
# The coverage manifest is generated on a developer's machine and diffed
# byte-for-byte on Linux CI, so it is the same trap as the entries above: a
# manifest regenerated on Windows would differ from CI's on every line at once
# and say "coverage changed" when nothing had. The generator also pins its own
# output to LF; this is the half that survives someone editing it by hand.
ci/coverage-manifest.txt text eol=lf
# Same trap, same fix: the notices are generated wherever a developer is and
# diffed byte-for-byte on Linux CI. The generator writes LF through
# `sys.stdout.buffer`; this is the half that survives a hand edit — which the
# file's own header asks nobody to make.
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md text eol=lf
